  "full_prompt": "Create an animation explaining limits in calculus. Start with the formal definition of the derivative as the limit of (f(x+h)-f(x))/h as h→0. Show that dx and df are concrete, finite nudges, and the derivative is what the ratio approaches as the nudge approaches zero. Then introduce the epsilon-delta definition of limits: for any epsilon (output tolerance), there exists a delta (input range) such that all inputs within delta of the target give outputs within epsilon of the limit. Show a counterexample where a limit does not exist (jump discontinuity). Finally, demonstrate L'Hôpital's rule: for functions f and g that both approach 0 at x=a, the limit of f/g equals the limit of f'/g', with the intuition that near a, each function is approximated by its tangent line, so the ratio of the functions equals the ratio of their derivatives.",
